      "text": "Given the system of congruences:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nabc \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}, \\\\\n5c \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}, \\\\\n6b \\equiv 3 + b \\pmod{7}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFirst, solve the third congruence:\n\\[\n6b \\equiv 3 + b \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nSubtract \\(b\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n5b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nTo solve for \\(b\\), we need the multiplicative inverse of 5 modulo 7. We find the inverse using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm:\n\\[\n7 = 1 \\cdot 5 + 2,\n\\]\n\\[\n5 = 2 \\cdot 2 + 1,\n\\]\n\\[\n2 = 2 \\cdot 1 + 0.\n\\]\nReversing the steps, we get:\n\\[\n1 = 5 - 2 \\cdot 2,\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = 5 - 2 \\cdot (7 - 1 \\cdot 5),\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = 3 \\cdot 5 - 2 \\cdot 7.\n\\]\nThus, the inverse of 5 modulo 7 is 3. Multiply both sides of \\(5b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}\\) by 3:\n\\[\nb \\equiv 3 \\cdot 3 \\pmod{7},\n\\]\n\\[\nb \\equiv 9 \\pmod{7},\n\\]\n\\[\nb \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\n\nNext, solve the second congruence:\n\\[\n5c \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nMultiply both sides by 3 (the inverse of 5 modulo 7):\n\\[\n15c \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7},\n\\]\n\\[\nc \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(b \\equiv 2\\) and \\(c \\equiv 6\\) into the first congruence:\n\\[\nabc \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\n\\[\na \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7},\n\\]\n\\[\n12a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7},\n\\]\n\\[\n5a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nTo solve \\(5a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\), find the inverse of 5 modulo 7. We already know the inverse is 3:\n\\[\na \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(a = 3\\), \\(b = 2\\), and \\(c = 6\\). Calculate \\(a + b + c\\):\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + 2 + 6 = 11.\n\\]\nFinally, find the remainder when 11 is divided by 7:\n\\[\n11 \\div 7 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 4.\n\\]\nTherefore, the remainder when \\(a + b + c\\) is divided by 7 is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
