      "text": "We start with the given congruences:\n\\[\n5c \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6b \\equiv 3 + b \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nFirst, let's solve for \\(c\\). We need to find the multiplicative inverse of 5 modulo 7. The inverse \\(x\\) satisfies:\n\\[\n5x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nBy testing values, we find that \\(x = 3\\) works because:\n\\[\n5 \\cdot 3 = 15 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nThus, we multiply both sides of the congruence \\(5c \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\) by 3:\n\\[\nc \\equiv 2 \\cdot 3 \\pmod{7} \\implies c \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nSo, \\(c = 6\\).\n\nNext, let's solve for \\(b\\). We re-write the second congruence:\n\\[\n6b \\equiv 3 + b \\pmod{7} \\implies 5b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nWe already know the inverse of 5 modulo 7 is 3, so we multiply both sides by 3:\n\\[\nb \\equiv 3 \\cdot 3 \\pmod{7} \\implies b \\equiv 9 \\pmod{7} \\implies b \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nSo, \\(b = 2\\).\n\nNow, we substitute \\(b = 2\\) and \\(c = 6\\) into the first congruence to solve for \\(a\\):\n\\[\na \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\implies 12a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\implies 5a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nWe already know the inverse of 5 modulo 7 is 3, so:\n\\[\na \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nSo, \\(a = 3\\).\n\nFinally, we need to find the remainder when \\(a + b + c\\) is divided by 7:\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + 2 + 6 = 11.\n\\]\nThe remainder when 11 is divided by 7 is:\n\\[\n11 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}.\n\\]\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}.\n\\]",
